    25	/* INTRODUCTION
    26	 * This very large dissector implements packet decoding for the entire
    27	 * protocol suite of the OpenDOF Project. The OpenDOF Project
    28	 * (https://opendof.org) is an open-source IoT platform with
    29	 * implementations in Java, C#, and C. The protocols are documented
    30	 * on the web site, and the IP ports referenced are registered with IANA.
    31	 *
    32	 * "DOF" stands for Distributed Object Framework. The protocols define
    33	 * a complete protocol stack that can sit on top of a variety of transports.
    34	 * The stack itself is called the DPS, or "DOF Protocol Stack". It
    35	 * is a layered stack including a Network, Presentation, and Application
    36	 * layer. The underlying transport can be anything, these dissectors
    37	 * hook in to UDP and TCP. To the Wireshark user, however, this is
    38	 * referred to as "dof" and not "dps".
    39	 *
    40	 * The following protocols are defined in the stack and implemented
    41	 * here:
    42	 *   DNP    - DOF Network Protocol (versions: 0, 1)
    43	 *   DPP    - DOF Presentation Protocol (version: 0, 2) [1 is reserved and not supported]
    44	 *   DAP    - DOF Application Protocols:
    45	 *   DSP    - DOF Session Protocol (versions: 0)
    46	 *   OAP    - Object Access Protocol (versions: 1)
    47	 *   TEP    - Ticket Exchange Protocol (versions: 128)
    48	 *   TRP    - Ticket Request Protocol (versions: 129)
    49	 *   SGMP   - Secure Group Management Protocol (versions: 130)
    50	 *   DOFSEC - DOF Security Protocols:
    51	 *   CCM    - Chained mode
    52	 *   TUN    - A tunneling protocol for embedding DOF in other protocols.
    53	 */
    54	
    55	/* VERSIONS AND NAMING
    56	 * There are several different ways in which "versions" are used
    57	 * throughout the dissector. First, each of the DNP and DPP layers
    58	 * has a defined 'version'. The DOF Application Protocols are also
    59	 * distinguished by versioning, but it is actually the registered
    60	 * application ID that is the version. This is complicated by
    61	 * the fact that many of the application IDs represent the same
    62	 * version of a protocol from a capability perspective (and
    63	 * a user perspective) with the difference being some attribute
    64	 * of the protocol - for example the security primitives used.
    65	 *
    66	 * Another means of versioning is by specification document.
    67	 * In this case the document is identified by a year and sequence,
    68	 * with specifications and PDUs using a name and sequence.
    69	 * Naming of fields and variables will use these identifiers
    70	 * as they are the easiest way to tie the code to the specifications.
    71	 *
    72	 * The specification documents are also the easiest way (although
    73	 * maybe not the clearest) to expose fields to the Wireshar user.
    74	 * A consistent naming is used, which is:
    75	 *
    76	 *   (spec)-pdu-(seq)-(field)
    77	 *   For example: dof-2009-1-pdu-1-value
    78	 *
    79	 * Variable naming includes a protocol name to provide clarity.
    80	 *
    81	 * This is not the clearest from a user perspective, but it
    82	 * has the benefit of tying directly to the specifications
    83	 * themselves and uniquely identifies each field.
    84	 *
    85	 * Routines that dissect are uniformly named by the PDU
    86	 * that they dissect using the PDU specification document
    87	 * and PDU name from that document as follows:
    88	 *
    89	 *   dissect_(spec)_(name)
    90	 */
