    92	/* DISSECTOR DESIGN
    93	 * The original work on these dissectors began over ten years ago, but
    94	 * shared only within Panasonic. During the opening of the protocols in
    95	 * March of 2015 the decision was made to contribute the code to the Wireshark
    96	 * community. During this process the plugin approach was rejected and the
    97	 * entire set made into standard dissectors, and further to that all of the
    98	 * dissectors were merged into a single file.
    99	 *
   100	 * There are several types of supported dissectors that are part of the DPS family.
   101	 * At the lowest level are the transport dissectors. The responsibility
   102	 * of these dissectors is to determine the transport session information, pass
   103	 * DPS packets to the DPS dissector, and properly maintain the dof_api_data
   104	 * structure.
   105	 *
   106	 * The DPS dissector API comprises:
   107	 *    1. The structure (dof_api_data) that is passed in the data field to the DPS
   108	 *       dissector. Transport plugins must understand this.
   109	 *    2. The dof_transport_session structure, which contains all transport
   110	 *       information that is passed to the DPS dissector.
   111	 *    3. The name of the DPS dissector.
   112	 *
   113	 * The DPS dissector API extends to dissectors that are called by the DPS dissectors.
   114	 *
   115	 * Finally, there is the DPS Security Mode dissectors. These dissectors are passed
   116	 * additional security context information and it is their job to decrypt packets
   117	 * and pass them to higher-level dissectors.
   118	 *
   119	 * The DOF Protocol Stack is strictly layered with minimal (and defined) state
   120	 * exchanged between layers. This allows a fairly structured design, using
   121	 * dissector tables at each layer. The main DPS dissector receives packets
   122	 * from the transport hooks, and then dissects the packet layer by layer using
   123	 * the different dissector tables. Dissectors and the DNP, DPP, and DAP layers.
   124	 *
   125	 * In addition to the main protocol stack with its associated protocols there are
   126	 * additional common data elements that include extensibility. If an extension
   127	 * is found then it will be used to dissect, otherwise the base dissector will be
   128	 * used.
   129	 */
   130	
   131	/* SESSIONS
   132	 * DOF defines sessions at many different levels, and state is always associated
   133	 * with a session. Much of the power (and complexity) of these dissectors relates
   134	 * to accurately tracking and maintaining context for each session, and displaying
   135	 * context-related decode information based on the session. This includes, for
   136	 * example, decoding encrypted data (including multicast group traffic) and
   137	 * showing full packet information even when the packet data uses aliases or
   138	 * other context specific data.
   139	 *
   140	 * Sessions are an extremely complex part of the dissectors because they occur at
   141	 * so many different levels, and that they are temporal in nature while wireshark
   142	 * is not. This means that all data structures that deal with sessions must deal
   143	 * with both the level and the time of the packet.
   144	 *
   145	 * The levels are:
   146	 *   1. Transport. These sessions are defined by the transport, and transport
   147	 *      addresses. As in the transports, there is no transport information allowed
   148	 *      at the dps level, but transport information is allowed to influence other
   149	 *      decisions. Every dps packet must be part of a transport session. Transport
   150	 *      sessions are usually managed as conversations in Wireshark. Each transport
   151	 *      session is has an identifier that is defined by the DPS plugin the first
   152	 *      time a packet in the transport session is passed to the plugin.
   153	 *   2. DPS. These sessions are defined by DPS, and are part of the DNP definition.
   154	 *      These sessions are also assigned a unique DPS session identifier.
   155	 *
   156	 *   3. Security (Optional). Security sessions always exist inside of
   157	 *      an DPS session. Security sessions are further divided into epochs, keys, etc.
   158	 *
   159	 * Temporal information is always associated with packet numbers, which always increase.
   160	 * This temporal information is used during the first pass to create sessions by
   161	 * determining that a new packet doesn't belong to a previous session.
   162	 *
   163	 * During the first pass the data structures are referenced from the transport
   164	 * session information up. The goal of the first pass is to create the most specific
   165	 * session information and associate each packet with the appropriate session. These
   166	 * sessions refer to more general session information.
   167	 *
   168	 * In order to make lookups easier, the most fine-grainded sessions are assigned
   169	 * unique identifiers. Secure sessions are always born unsecure (during security
   170	 * negotiation). These use the same session identifiers, but the state for the
   171	 * secure and unsecured times are separated. Once a session is secured it never
   172	 * transitions back to unsecured.
   173	 *
   174	 * MEMBERSHIP
   175	 * Each packet is sent by a member of the session. Session members have state related
   176	 * to the session. Packets are received by either a member of the session or the
   177	 * session itself (implying all members). This means that packet state can come
   178	 * from:
   179	 *   1. The sender.
   180	 *   2. The receiver (if directed to a receiver).
   181	 *   3. The session.
   182	 * The identity of a member is always a combination of transport and dps information.
   183	 * However, the state of the membership is in the context of the session, keyed by
   184	 * the sender.
   185	 *
   186	 * In order to make lookups easier, each unique sender in the system is
   187	 * assigned a unique identifier.
   188	 */
